Wait, I'm running into a lifetime problem here. The program AST borrows from the allocator, but both the allocator and source string are local variables that will be dropped. The semantic analysis also depends on the program, which creates a chain of borrows that won't outlive the function scope.

Actually, looking at what `segment_file` returns—it's `Vec<Segment>`, which is fully owned. The segments contain spans as simple `u32` values and names as `Box<str>`, so there are no borrowed references being returned. This should work fine since everything in the returned vector is owned data.

Let me verify what's actually in a `Span`—it's just start and end positions as integers, no lifetimes involved. I need to be careful about the declaration order — `source` should come before `allocator` so that when things go out of scope, `source` is dropped last (since `ret` borrows from it). I'm also checking the `WrapFileLogger::new` signature to see how it handles the `source` parameter.

The issue is that `parse_file` borrows `source` as a reference, but `WrapFileLogger::new` wants to take ownership of it as a `String`. I can't move `source` into the logger while `ret` still holds a borrow to it, so I'll need to either clone the source for the logger or restructure how the ownership flows.

Since `segment_file` processes everything synchronously and returns fully owned data, I can read the file into a `String`, pass a reference to it for parsing, then clone that string for the logger after parsing is complete.

Setting up the code structure... I see the real solution now — I can create the logger before doing any parsing, so it owns the source string independently and there's no borrow conflict with the allocator and semantic analyzer that need the same source during parsing. The old code worked with a reference to the `SourceFile` that already owned the source text, so it didn't consume anything. The cleanest path forward is just to clone the string—it's a one-time cost per file parse, and given that the source is already loaded in memory, it's a reasonable trade-off. I could explore `Arc<String>` or restructure things, but cloning keeps this straightforward.

Now I need to handle parse errors properly. The oxc parser can produce errors while still recovering and continuing, but for `get_file_segments` we should treat any parse errors as a failure, matching the original behavior where parse failures returned a `TypeScriptSyntax` error. So I'll check that `ret.errors.is_empty()` to ensure a clean parse.

